0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.09$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.09}{0.06}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(1.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.20$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.20}{0.05}=4.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.5)^{p+1} & ≈4 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{1.5}⁡(4) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.419… \\\\ p & ≈2.419….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443219 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443230 1069 "q-334739" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443230 1071 "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=0.4Δx+5(Δx)^{2}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=7(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{4}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{2}+0.2(Δx)^{3}$" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443230 1072 #uuid "2d0aee14-de07-5ea6-8cb4-30e573b28550" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443230 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.4Δx+5(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4Δx.$ So, statement I corresponds to