ocal truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443234 1072 #uuid "a4879a58-4027-569c-b7a3-4f905f12c0fb" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443234 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.6) & =4(0.6)^{3}+(0.6)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.216+0.129\\,6 \\\\  & =0.993\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.994.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.2) & =4(0.2)^{3}+(0.2)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.008+0.001\\,6 \\\\  & =0.033\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.034.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={3}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.993\\,6$ to $0.033\\,6.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.993\\,6}{0.033\\,6}=29.571\\,42…≈29.571.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈29.571\\,42… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(29.571\\,42…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.083… \\\\ p & ≈2.083….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$2.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+(Δx)^{4}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{3})=O\\,((Δx)^{2+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $2.$" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443207 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443210 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443214 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443227 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443235 1069 "q-334561" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443235 1071 "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.4$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443235 1072 #uuid "a67eecd4-7182-53b5-9971-e13bae152b30" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443235 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.4) & =5(0.4)^{4}+(0.4)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.0256+1\\cdot 0.010\\,24 \\\\  & =0.128+0.010\\,24 \\\\  & =0.138\\,24 \\\\  & ≈0.138.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =5(0.2)^{4}+(0.2)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.0016+1\\cdot 0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.008+0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.008\\,32 \\\\  & ≈0.008.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.4}{0.2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.138\\,24$ to $0.008\\,32.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.138\\,24}{0.008\\,32}=16.615\\,384…≈16.615.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈16.615\\,384… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(16.615\\,384…) \\\\ p & ≈4.054….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443225 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443236 1069 "q-334541" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443236 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.10)=0.196.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.05,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.05)=0.050.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443236 1072 #