nteger such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{1+1}={3}^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{0.27}{9}=0.03.\n$$" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443211 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443215 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443223 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443228 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443238 1069 "q-334668" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443238 1071 "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.3,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443238 1072 #uuid "d97bcc9d-8c7b-5d3a-9dd6-550b8b7b1d07" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443238 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.6) & =5(0.6)^{4}+3(0.6)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.129\\,6+3\\cdot 0.077\\,76 \\\\  & =0.648+0.233\\,28 \\\\  & =0.881\\,28 \\\\  & ≈0.881.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.3) & =5(0.3)^{4}+3(0.3)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.008\\,1+3\\cdot 0.002\\,43 \\\\  & =0.0405+0.007\\,29 \\\\  & =0.047\\,79 \\\\  & ≈0.048.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.3.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.3}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.881\\,28$ to $0.047\\,79.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.881\\,28}{0.047\\,79}=18.440\\,67…≈18.441.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈18.440\\,67… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(18.440\\,67…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.204… \\\\ p & ≈3.204….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})=O\\,((Δx)^{3+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443226 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443239 1069 "q-334750" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443239 1071 "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n\nI. $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+2(Δx)^{5}$\n\nII. $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3}$\n\nIII. $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+9(Δx)^{4}$" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443239 1072 #uuid "dd55dc02-570c-5acd-bec7-3aaa43923830" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443239 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+2(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{2}=0.4(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+9(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{2}=5(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443206 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443213 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443218 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443224 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443240 1069 "q-334564" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443240 1071 "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.3,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443240 1072 #uuid "ec94fc3e-e8fc-535e-a62b-74b6f854910e" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true] [17592186443240 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.6) & =6(0.6)^{3}-(0.6)^{4} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.216-1\\cdot 0.129\\,6 \\\\  & =1.296-0.129\\,6 \\\\  & =1.166\\,4 \\\\  & ≈1.166.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.3) & =6(0.3)^{3}-(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.027-1\\cdot 0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.162-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.153\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.154.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.3.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.3}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $1.166\\,4$ to $0.153\\,9.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{1.166\\,4}{0.153\\,9}=7.578\\,94…≈7.579.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈7.578\\,94… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(7.578\\,94…) \\\\ p & ≈2.922….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{3})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$" 13194139534898 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-043a9000-9408-5bcf-93fc-497849ff02db" 17592186443092 "answer-0a508b9f-0d5a-531f-825c-e1abd93fb8bb" 17592186443093 "answer-0b656bb6-b726-513c-9a3d-224438a3ecc4" 17592186443094 "answer-11681063-6b8a-56d3-bde1-2daa1f9bda0f" 17592186443095 "answer-11d03973-840a-5f94-b34c-89b7978bd218" 17592186443096 "answer-1629f72c-2b7a-5ad9-9802-4a4c18a43f8a" 17592186443097 "answer-16811bb1-a290-5110-9278-74a13897fa65" 17592186443098 "answer-1a446e26-74cb-5835-b369-58a822cab3a1" 17592186443099 "answer-1a7dd00c-ee32-5ddb-b80a-7e15b11f8bf1" 17592186443100 "answer-1ca02d59-9dac-5356-af9b-e15310a7e10e" 17592186443101 "answer-218a2453-01ac-5477-9e84-010ad313a737" 17592186443102 "answer-21e74dca-8634-5f9d-b911-c298c57cfbc2" 17592186443103 "answer-2292995f-4795-53f0-bb31-0814de610887" 17592186443104 "answer-29061ed8-03ee-5954-99b1-36ee8a7d846e" 17592186443105 "answer-2d85bffd-8755-5e9b-a7b3-89febd886899" 17592186443106 "answer-34e9b221-f1ec-557c-a529-86f750142cb5" 17592186443107 "answer-36fa518b-6582-5224-a8cb-c3138d904c19" 17592186443108 "answer-387f547e-35bc-53a4-9409-9ca8ed35c9b3" 17592186443109 "answer-38bad0d5-2ff2-5723-811f-1db1ba0d66dd" 17592186443110 "answer-3c888821-4abd-556d-946d-6732bb9e8b80" 17592186443111 "answer-3ceeb595-482c-56a6-b69a-748520742b40" 17592186443112 "answer-3ff78622-07cd-5b38-9b4f-d965d43b678c" 17592186443113 "answer-40cae3ae-8778-572c-b151-41eb5027ec6c" 17592186443114 "answer-40e22b30-d2e7-587d-9946-51d20145fa00" 17592186443115 "answer-418b4194-ffcb-5bd9-80c5-0907e534d558" 17592186443116 "answe